I also encountered this bug today. My example is not exactly simple either, but I tried to remove as much as possible. The original purpose of this snippet got lost by simplifying, but the bug is still triggered

# Broken
`ShapeError: unable to vstack, column names don't match: "key" and "value"`
```python
import polars as pl

df1 = pl.DataFrame(
    {
        "key": [1],
        "x": [1],
    }
).lazy()

df2 = pl.DataFrame(
    {
        "key": [1],
        "y": [2],
    }
).lazy()

joined_df2 = df1.join(df2, on="key")

all_subsections = (
    pl.concat([
        df1.select("key", pl.col("x").alias("value")),
        joined_df2.select("key", pl.col("y").alias("value")),
    ])
    .groupby("key")
    .agg(
        [
            pl.col("value"),
        ]
    )
)

intersected_df1 = all_subsections.join(df1, on="key")
intersected_df2 = all_subsections.join(df2, on="key")

intersected_df1.join(intersected_df2, on=["key"], how="left").collect()
```

# Works
```python
import polars as pl

df1 = pl.DataFrame(
    {
        "key": [1],
        "x": [1],
    }
).lazy()

df2 = pl.DataFrame(
    {
        "key": [1],
        "y": [2],
    }
).lazy()

joined_df2 = df1.join(df2, on="key").collect().lazy()

all_subsections = (
    pl.concat([
        df1.select("key", pl.col("x").alias("value")),
        joined_df2.select("key", pl.col("y").alias("value")),
    ])
    .groupby("key")
    .agg(
        [
            pl.col("value"),
        ]
    )
)

intersected_df1 = all_subsections.join(df1, on="key")
intersected_df2 = all_subsections.join(df2, on="key")

intersected_df1.join(intersected_df2, on=["key"], how="left").collect()
```
